      <!-- Legal basis -->
      <section id="legal-basis">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-10 mb-4">Legal basis</h2>
        <p class="text-text-secondary leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Collection and publication take place in France (EU), where the
          maintainer carries out this work as a researcher within the Neuropresage
          Team (Normandie Université, UNICAEN, INSERM, PhIND, UMR-S U1237, Caen).
          The basis for the two distinct activities — gathering the pages, and
          holding personal data about the people on them — is as follows:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 text-text-secondary space-y-2 text-sm mb-4">
          <li>
            <strong>Collection (text-and-data mining).</strong> Editorial-board
            pages are gathered under the <strong>scientific-research
            text-and-data-mining exception</strong> introduced by Article 3 of the
            EU Copyright-in-the-Digital-Single-Market Directive
            (2019/790) and transposed into French law in the
            <em>Code de la propriété intellectuelle</em>
            (<strong>Art. L.122-5-3</strong> for copyright and
            <strong>Art. L.342-3</strong> for the <em>sui generis</em> database
            right). It permits reproduction and extraction, for the purposes of
            scientific research, of works and databases to which a research
            organisation has lawful access; it applies to research organisations,
            cannot be overridden by contract, and here covers only publicly
            accessible pages.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Facts are not copyrightable — but databases can be
            protected.</strong> The content the dataset actually retains — names,
            roles, affiliations — is factual and not protected by copyright.
            Unlike some jurisdictions, the EU also recognises a <em>sui
            generis</em> database right over publishers' compilations; extracting
            from them for scientific research is nonetheless permitted by the
            TDM exception cited above (Art. L.342-3), so the extraction is not
            restricted on database-right grounds.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Public pages only.</strong> Only publicly accessible
            editorial-board pages are used; no login-gated, paywalled, or licensed
            content is collected.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Personal data (GDPR).</strong> Because the records identify
            people, the processing is governed by the EU General Data Protection
            Regulation (2016/679) and the French <em>loi Informatique et
            Libertés</em>. The data — professional information about people acting
            in a public scholarly role — are processed for scientific research on
            the basis of the controller's legitimate interests
            (<strong>Art. 6(1)(f)</strong>) together with the research safeguards
            of <strong>Art. 89(1)</strong>; sex/gender is not special-category
            data under Art. 9. Full details, data-subject rights, and the
            supervisory authority (the French CNIL) are set out in the
            privacy notice.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
          The full data-protection notice — named data controller, contact,
          inferred-gender caveat, licence limits, and how to correct or remove a
          record — is on the
          <a href="/privacy" class="text-brand-600 hover:underline">Privacy &amp; Data Protection</a>
          page.
        </p>
      </section>

